            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Fifth Circuit previously held that DAPA and the expansion of DACA were likely unlawful, and the Supreme Court affirmed that judgment in an equally divided decision."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Department of Homeland Security",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6462e834951c2781b570be865b133f4f2b2736c67fb80dc0a6c73d1efb857c9f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6462e834951c-0000-0002"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 5,
              "start": 5
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The Department of Homeland Security argues that it no longer wishes to retain the DACA policy because it believes the policy is illegal, has serious doubts about its legality, and opposes broad non\u2011enforcement policies."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Private Respondents",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6462e834951c2781b570be865b133f4f2b2736c67fb80dc0a6c73d1efb857c9f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6462e834951c-0002-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 44,
              "start": 44
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Private respondents contend that the government's termination of DACA caused abrupt, tangible, adverse consequences and substantial disruptions in the lives of about 700,000 individuals, their families, employers, communities, and the Armed Forces."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Private Respondents",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6462e834951c2781b570be865b133f4f2b2736c67fb80dc0a6c73d1efb857c9f",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6462e834951c-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 45,
              "start": 45
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Private Respondents, the Attorney General asserted that he had no discretion because DACA was an unconstitutional exercise of executive authority."
      }
